<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Background</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Loop Nest Notation in Neptune IR.</span>
Program snippets in Figures <a href="#S2.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2. Motivation ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>-<a href="#S2.F1.sf3" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2. Motivation ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(c)</span></a>
show examples of loops in Neptune’s loop-scalar IR.
A loop in this IR is defined by an iteration variable, an upper bound, and a body.
Neptune normalizes the range of loops to start from <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> without loss of generality.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In equations, we use a compact notation for loop nests in this IR: <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nest(\boldsymbol{i}:\boldsymbol{n})\{s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝒏</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nest(\boldsymbol{i}:\boldsymbol{n})\{s\}</annotation></semantics></math>
to represent <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> perfectly nested loops with iteration variables <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}:=(i_{1},\dots,i_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒊</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}:=(i_{1},\dots,i_{K})</annotation></semantics></math>
and upper bounds <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}:=(n_{1},\dots,n_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒏</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}:=(n_{1},\dots,n_{K})</annotation></semantics></math>, and loop body <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="nest((i_{1},i_{2}):(n_{1},n_{2}))\{seq(s_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nest((i_{1},i_{2}):(n_{1},n_{2}))\{seq(s_{1},</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A2.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{2})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2})\}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to</p>
<div id="A2.p2.2" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_python ltx_lstlisting ltx_listing"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64,Zm9yIGkxIGluIHJhbmdlKG4xKToKICBmb3IgaTIgaW4gcmFuZ2UobjIpOgogICAgczE7IHMy" download="">⬇</a></div>
<div id="lstnumberx116" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx116.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_bold">for</span><span id="lstnumberx116.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx116.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i1</span><span id="lstnumberx116.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx116.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_bold">in</span><span id="lstnumberx116.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx116.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_lst_keywords2 ltx_font_bold">range</span>(<span id="lstnumberx116.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">n1</span>):
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx117" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx117.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space">  </span><span id="lstnumberx117.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_bold">for</span><span id="lstnumberx117.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx117.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i2</span><span id="lstnumberx117.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx117.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_bold">in</span><span id="lstnumberx117.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx117.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_lst_keywords2 ltx_font_bold">range</span>(<span id="lstnumberx117.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">n2</span>):
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx118" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx118.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space">    </span><span id="lstnumberx118.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">s1</span>;<span id="lstnumberx118.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx118.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">s2</span>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒊</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is the iteration vector of the loop nest.
Iteration vectors from the same loop nest are comparable via the lexicographic order:
<math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{1}\prec\boldsymbol{i}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≺</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{1}\prec\boldsymbol{i}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is lexicographically less than <math id="A2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="A2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the upper bound of the loop nest.
The iteration domain of the loop nest is an axis-aligned box between <math id="A2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="A2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{s}=\{\boldsymbol{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{K}\mid 0\preceq\boldsymbol{i}\preceq\boldsymbol{n}\}=:\operatorname{box}(0,\boldsymbol{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝒊</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>⪯</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo>⪯</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒏</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{s}=\{\boldsymbol{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{K}\mid 0\preceq\boldsymbol{i}\preceq\boldsymbol{n}\}=:\operatorname{box}(0,\boldsymbol{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We adopt the closed interval convention when discussing iteration vectors.
We denote the previous iteration of <math id="A2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{prev}(\boldsymbol{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>prev</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{prev}(\boldsymbol{i})</annotation></semantics></math>,
which is the maximum of all iteration vectors smaller than <math id="A2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="A2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{prev}(\boldsymbol{i}):=\operatorname{lexmax}(\{\boldsymbol{i}^{\prime}\in\mathcal{D}^{s}\mid\boldsymbol{i}^{\prime}\prec\boldsymbol{i}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>prev</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>lexmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒊</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒊</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≺</mo><mi>𝒊</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{prev}(\boldsymbol{i}):=\operatorname{lexmax}(\{\boldsymbol{i}^{\prime}\in\mathcal{D}^{s}\mid\boldsymbol{i}^{\prime}\prec\boldsymbol{i}\})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, <math id="A2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{next}(\boldsymbol{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>next</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{next}(\boldsymbol{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the next iteration of <math id="A2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We may also omit the upper bounds and the body of the loop nest if unused in the context,
so <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nest(\boldsymbol{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nest(\boldsymbol{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nest(\boldsymbol{i})\{s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nest(\boldsymbol{i})\{s\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nest(\boldsymbol{i}:\boldsymbol{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝒏</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nest(\boldsymbol{i}:\boldsymbol{n})</annotation></semantics></math>
are valid notations for a loop nest.
We refer to a loop in a given loop nest by its iteration variable:
<math id="A2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nest(\boldsymbol{i}:\boldsymbol{n})\{s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝒏</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nest(\boldsymbol{i}:\boldsymbol{n})\{s\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="A2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th loop in the nest.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Affine Access Function and Tags.</span>
Array loads and stores in Neptune IR use affine access functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>)</cite>
to index array elements.
In an array access <math id="A2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X[\phi(\boldsymbol{i})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X[\phi(\boldsymbol{i})]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is the array being accessed,
and <math id="A2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is the affine access function
that projects the iteration vector <math id="A2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to array indices.
Since <math id="A2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is affine, we can fully describe it with a matrix <math id="A2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and a vector <math id="A2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\mathbb{Z}^{N}\to\mathbb{Z}^{M}\quad\phi(\boldsymbol{i})=A\boldsymbol{i}+b" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒊</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\mathbb{Z}^{N}\to\mathbb{Z}^{M}\quad\phi(\boldsymbol{i})=A\boldsymbol{i}+b</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is not injective in general.
For example, in Figure <a href="#S2.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2. Motivation ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>, the array store <span id="A2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">xsum[i]</span>
has an access function of <math id="A2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(i,j)=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(i,j)=i</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is then difficult to refer to the value of <span id="A2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">xsum[i]</span> at different <math id="A2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-iterations.
In such cases, we add a <span id="A2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tag</span> to the array access
to mark which <math id="A2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-iteration the access is referring to:
<math id="A2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X[\phi(\boldsymbol{i})]^{\langle j\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X[\phi(\boldsymbol{i})]^{\langle j\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{{xsum}[i]}^{\langle j\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">xsum[i]</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{{xsum}[i]}^{\langle j\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> for the example above).
Tags reflect temporal data dependency on the same memory location.
They convey the same idea as Figure <a href="#S2.F1.sf5" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2. Motivation ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(e)</span></a>
duplicating the arrays <span id="A2.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">xsum</span> and <span id="A2.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">xmax</span> to show their value over time.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reduce Loop Nest.</span>
A reduce loop nest is a loop nest that expresses a reduction operation.
In Neptune IR, a reduce loop nest consists of map loops and reduce loops,
where the output is only indexed by the map loop variables,
and each output value is updated as many times as the reduce loops execute.
For example, the <span id="A2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">s_sum</span> loop nest in Figure <a href="#S2.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2. Motivation ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>
is a reduce loop nest, with one map loop <math id="A2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and one reduce loop <math id="A2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
The output array <span id="A2.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">xsum</span> is only indexed by <math id="A2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and each value of <span id="A2.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">xsum[i]</span> is updated 4 times.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To distinguish reduce loops and map loops,
we use the specialized loop nest notation <math id="A2.p8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="rnest(\boldsymbol{i}:\boldsymbol{n},\boldsymbol{j}:\boldsymbol{m})\{s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝒏</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒋</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝒎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rnest(\boldsymbol{i}:\boldsymbol{n},\boldsymbol{j}:\boldsymbol{m})\{s\}</annotation></semantics></math> for a reduce loop nest.
Therefore, a reduce loop nest has the form</p>
<table id="A2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
