<p id="A2.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is the reducer (an associative binary function),
<math id="A2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\ \hbox to10.43pt{\vbox to10.43pt{\pgfpicture\makeatletter\hbox{\hskip 5.21538pt\lower-5.21538pt\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke=#000000} \lxSVG@begingroup@{fill=#000000} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.4pt} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nullfont\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} {{{}}\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\hbox{\hbox{{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} {{}{{{}}}{{}}{}{}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{}{}{}{}{}{{}\lxSVG@stroke\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 6.94 0 C 6.94 3.83 3.83 6.94 0 6.94 C -3.83 6.94 -6.94 3.83 -6.94 0 C -6.94 -3.83 -3.83 -6.94 0 -6.94 C 3.83 -6.94 6.94 -3.83 6.94 0 Z M 0 0}{fill:none} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces
}{{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@transformcm{1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{1.0}{-2.48265pt}{-2.0pt}\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform=matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.44 -2.77)} \pgfsys@hbox{58}\lxSVG@closescope }}}
\lxSVG@closescope }}}
}
\lxSVG@closescope {{{}}}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}\hss}\lxSVG@discardpath\lxSVG@closescope \hss}}\lxSVG@closescope\endpgfpicture}}\ b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> <svg style="vertical-align:-4.45px" height="14.43" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 14.43 14.43" width="14.43"> <g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,14.43) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(7.22,0) translate(0,7.22)"> <path style="fill:none" d="M 6.94 0 C 6.94 3.83 3.83 6.94 0 6.94 C -3.83 6.94 -6.94 3.83 -6.94 0 C -6.94 -3.83 -3.83 -6.94 0 -6.94 C 3.83 -6.94 6.94 -3.83 6.94 0 Z M 0 0"></path> <g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.44 -2.77)"> <foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.58em;--ltx-fo-height:0.65em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:8.5pt;" height="9.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.69)" width="6.87"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi></span></span></foreignObject> </g> </g> </svg> </mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\ \hbox to10.43pt{\vbox to10.43pt{\pgfpicture\makeatletter\hbox{\hskip 5.21538pt\lower-5.21538pt\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke=#000000} \lxSVG@begingroup@{fill=#000000} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.4pt} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nullfont\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} {{{}}\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\hbox{\hbox{{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} {{}{{{}}}{{}}{}{}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{}{}{}{}{}{{}\lxSVG@stroke\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 6.94 0 C 6.94 3.83 3.83 6.94 0 6.94 C -3.83 6.94 -6.94 3.83 -6.94 0 C -6.94 -3.83 -3.83 -6.94 0 -6.94 C 3.83 -6.94 6.94 -3.83 6.94 0 Z M 0 0}{fill:none} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces
}{{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@transformcm{1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{1.0}{-2.48265pt}{-2.0pt}\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform=matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.44 -2.77)} \pgfsys@hbox{58}\lxSVG@closescope }}}
\lxSVG@closescope }}}
}
\lxSVG@closescope {{{}}}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}\hss}\lxSVG@discardpath\lxSVG@closescope \hss}}\lxSVG@closescope\endpgfpicture}}\ b</annotation></semantics></math> is the infix form of <math id="A2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="A2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="expr(\boldsymbol{i},\boldsymbol{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒋</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">expr(\boldsymbol{i},\boldsymbol{j})</annotation></semantics></math> is the values being reduced over that depend on <math id="A2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recurrent and Explicit Forms.</span>
Reduce loop nests in Neptune that match Equation <a href="#A2.E9" title="In Appendix B Background ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>
updates the output array <math id="A2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> iteratively.
We refer to Equation <a href="#A2.E9" title="In Appendix B Background ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> as the <span id="A2.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">recurrent form</span>
for the value of <math id="A2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is useful to have a closed-form expression for it.
We define the reduce operator <math id="A2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> that accepts 3 arguments:
a reducer <math id="A2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, a range to reduce over, and the expression to reduce over.
For instance, <math id="A2.p9.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{R}(+,0\leq j\leq 2,\texttt{xexp}[i,j])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">xexp</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(+,0\leq j\leq 2,\texttt{xexp}[i,j])</annotation></semantics></math> equals
<math id="A2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{xexp}[i,0]+\texttt{xexp}[i,1]+\texttt{xexp}[i,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">xexp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">xexp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">xexp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{xexp}[i,0]+\texttt{xexp}[i,1]+\texttt{xexp}[i,2]</annotation></semantics></math>.
We refer to this reduce operator-based expression as the <span id="A2.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">explicit form</span>
for the value of <math id="A2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataflow Analysis.</span>
Neptune performs dataflow analysis at the loop nest level.
A loop nest <math id="A2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="A2.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">producer</span> of another loop nest <math id="A2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
if <math id="A2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> writes to arrays that <math id="A2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> reads from,
and <math id="A2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="A2.p10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">consumer</span> of <math id="A2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
Producer-consumer relation between loop nests forms a graph, which we call the dataflow graph.
A loop nest <math id="A2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a predecessor of <math id="A2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is reachable from <math id="A2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on the dataflow graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Naive Loop Fusion.</span>
Loop fusion is a standard transformation in compiler literature
that fuses multiple loop nests into a single nest.
Neptune uses the Halide-style <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>)</cite> loop fusion algorithm (known as compute-at in Halide).
We modify its data dependency checks such that it no longer validates temporal (tag) data dependency.
Data dependency checks based on memory location is still in effect.
This modified algorithm is the <span id="A2.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">naive loop fusion</span> algorithm used in Neptune.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Naive loop fusion takes a loop nest <math id="A2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}:=nest(\boldsymbol{i})\{s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}:=nest(\boldsymbol{i})\{s\}</annotation></semantics></math> to fuse
and a loop <math id="A2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> to fuse <math id="A2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> under: <span id="A2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NaiveLoopFusion(<math id="A2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,<math id="A2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>.
<math id="A2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> and any loops outside of it are together denoted as a loop nest <math id="A2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
Naive loop fusion moves <math id="A2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="A2.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, and cuts the loop ranges of <math id="A2.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
based on the ranges of <math id="A2.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and memory location dependency.
The modified loop nest <math id="A2.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> sits under <math id="A2.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and still contains the statement <math id="A2.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.
We refer to <math id="A2.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> as the outer loop nest and <math id="A2.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as the remainder loop nest of the fusion.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Array Tile.</span>
A tile of an array is a multi-dimensional sub-array.
For an array <math id="A2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions and shape <math id="A2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1},\dots,m_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1},\dots,m_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>,
a tile of <math id="A2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an axis-aligned box <math id="A2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[l_{1},u_{1})\times\dots\times[l_{K},u_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[l_{1},u_{1})\times\dots\times[l_{K},u_{K})</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="A2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{k},u_{k}\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{k},u_{k}\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq l_{k}&lt;u_{k}&lt;m_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq l_{k}&lt;u_{k}&lt;m_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
A tile of <math id="A2.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> may have fewer or more dimensions than <math id="A2.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Neptune’s tile IR has a syntax for removing and inserting dimensions of size 1 on the tile.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Step-By-Step Algorithm Application on Motivational Example</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we include the detailed steps of our rolling update algorithm for the example from Figure <a href="#S2.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2. Motivation ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1. </span>Rolling Update</h3>

