</div>
<div id="lstnumberx154" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx154.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space">    </span><span id="lstnumberx154.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">xmax_1</span>[<span id="lstnumberx154.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i</span>]<span id="lstnumberx154.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>=<span id="lstnumberx154.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx154.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_lst_keywords2 ltx_font_bold">max</span>(<span id="lstnumberx154.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">xmax_1</span>[<span id="lstnumberx154.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i</span>],<span id="lstnumberx154.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx154.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">xmax_1p</span>[<span id="lstnumberx154.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i</span>,<span id="lstnumberx154.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx154.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">j1</span>])
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx155" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx155.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space">    </span><span id="lstnumberx155.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">xsump</span>[<span id="lstnumberx155.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i</span>,<span id="lstnumberx155.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx155.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">j1</span>]<span id="lstnumberx155.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>=<span id="lstnumberx155.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx155.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_lst_keywords2 ltx_font_bold">reduce</span>(+,<span id="lstnumberx155.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx155.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">dim</span>=1,
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx156" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx156.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space">      </span><span id="lstnumberx156.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">exp</span>(<span id="lstnumberx156.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">inputs</span>[<span id="lstnumberx156.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i</span>,<span id="lstnumberx156.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx156.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">j1</span>*2<span id="lstnumberx156.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>:<span id="lstnumberx156.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx156.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">j1</span>*2+2]<span id="lstnumberx156.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>-<span id="lstnumberx156.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx156.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">xmax_1</span>[<span id="lstnumberx156.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i</span>]))
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx157" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx157.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space">    </span><span id="lstnumberx157.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">xsum</span>[<span id="lstnumberx157.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i</span>]<span id="lstnumberx157.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>=<span id="lstnumberx157.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>(
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx158" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx158.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space">      </span><span id="lstnumberx158.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">exp</span>(<span id="lstnumberx158.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">xmax_0</span>[<span id="lstnumberx158.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i</span>]<span id="lstnumberx158.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>-<span id="lstnumberx158.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx158.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">xmax_1</span>[<span id="lstnumberx158.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i</span>])
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx159" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx159.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space">      </span>*<span id="lstnumberx159.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx159.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">xsum</span>[<span id="lstnumberx159.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i</span>]<span id="lstnumberx159.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>+<span id="lstnumberx159.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx159.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">xsump</span>[<span id="lstnumberx159.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i</span>,<span id="lstnumberx159.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx159.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">j1</span>]
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx160" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx160.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space">    </span>)
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx161" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx161.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space">    </span><span id="lstnumberx161.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">xmax_0</span>[<span id="lstnumberx161.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i</span>]<span id="lstnumberx161.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>=<span id="lstnumberx161.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx161.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">xmax_1</span>[<span id="lstnumberx161.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">i</span>]
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F14.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 14</span>. </span><span id="A5.F14.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The result of tensorizing the program in Figure <a href="#A3.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ C.2. Privatization ‣ Appendix C Step-By-Step Algorithm Application on Motivational Example ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AST Partitioning.</span>
AST partitioning selects subtrees on the input AST
that do not include sequential statements (<math id="A5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="seq\_stmt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">seq\_stmt</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#A4.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ Appendix D Proofs of Rolling Update Correctness Theorems ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)
and annotated parallel loops (e.g. <span id="A5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">blockIdx.x i in range(32)</span>).
Sequential statements and loop annotations do not have a counterpart in tile expressions,
so they are excluded from tensorization.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Partitioning works bottom-up from store statements.
A store statement <math id="A5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is a leaf statement in the loop-scalar IR
and has loop parents <math id="A5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}:=(i_{1},\dots,i_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒊</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}:=(i_{1},\dots,i_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> from the root of the AST.
For each <math id="A5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, partitioning finds the outermost loop <math id="A5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{p}\in\boldsymbol{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒊</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{p}\in\boldsymbol{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that the whole subtree <math id="A5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is structurally tensorizable.
We denote this partitioned AST around <math id="A5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A5.p5.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="part(\boldsymbol{i}_{o}:\boldsymbol{n}_{o},\boldsymbol{i}_{i}:\boldsymbol{n}_{i})\{s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">part(\boldsymbol{i}_{o}:\boldsymbol{n}_{o},\boldsymbol{i}_{i}:\boldsymbol{n}_{i})\{s\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="A5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{o}:=(i_{1},\dots,i_{p-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{o}:=(i_{1},\dots,i_{p-1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{i}:=(i_{p},\dots,i_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{i}:=(i_{p},\dots,i_{K})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The statement <math id="A5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and inner loops <math id="A5.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> form a perfect loop nest <math id="A5.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nest(\boldsymbol{i}_{i}:\boldsymbol{n}_{i})\{s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nest(\boldsymbol{i}_{i}:\boldsymbol{n}_{i})\{s\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the input program Figure <a href="#A3.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ C.2. Privatization ‣ Appendix C Step-By-Step Algorithm Application on Motivational Example ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>,
since the body of the loop <math id="A5.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequential statement,
partitioning stops at <math id="A5.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and returns <math id="A5.p5.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="part((i,j_{1}):(2,2),(j_{2}):(2))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">part((i,j_{1}):(2,2),(j_{2}):(2))</annotation></semantics></math>
for stores <span id="A5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max_local</span> and <span id="A5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sum_local</span>.
The partitions of other stores have empty inner loops.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Access Function Relaxation.</span>

For each AST partition <math id="A5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="part(\boldsymbol{i}_{o}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">part(\boldsymbol{i}_{o},</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}_{o},\boldsymbol{i}_{i},\boldsymbol{n}_{i})\{s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}_{o},\boldsymbol{i}_{i},\boldsymbol{n}_{i})\{s\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
access function relaxation converts each array access of <math id="A5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> into a tile access (producing <math id="A5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>),
such that the region <math id="A5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> accesses in <math id="A5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> iterations is the same
as the region accessed by <math id="A5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in one iteration.
We assume that <math id="A5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> has the following form:</p>
<table id="A5.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}[\phi_{0}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o},\boldsymbol{i}_{i})]=F(X_{1}[\phi_{1}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o},\boldsymbol{i}_{i})],\dots,X_{K}[\phi_{K}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o},\boldsymbol{i}_{i})])" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}[\phi_{0}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o},\boldsymbol{i}_{i})]=F(X_{1}[\phi_{1}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o},\boldsymbol{i}_{i})],\dots,X_{K}[\phi_{K}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o},\boldsymbol{i}_{i})])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Relaxation finds the range of each <math id="A5.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> over the inner iteration domain <math id="A5.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and checks if this range is a tile that fits in the tile syntax of the IR.
We now define these notions precisely.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\boldsymbol{i}_{1},\boldsymbol{i}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\boldsymbol{i}_{1},\boldsymbol{i}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> be an affine access function</p>
<table id="A5.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\mathcal{D}_{1}\times\mathcal{D}_{2}\to\mathbb{Z}^{M}\quad\phi(\boldsymbol{i}_{1},\boldsymbol{i}_{2})=U\boldsymbol{i}_{1}+V\boldsymbol{i}_{2}+b" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\mathcal{D}_{1}\times\mathcal{D}_{2}\to\mathbb{Z}^{M}\quad\phi(\boldsymbol{i}_{1},\boldsymbol{i}_{2})=U\boldsymbol{i}_{1}+V\boldsymbol{i}_{2}+b</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{1}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{N_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{1}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{N_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{2}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{N_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{2}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{N_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathbb{Z}^{M\times N_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathbb{Z}^{M\times N_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\in\mathbb{Z}^{M\times N_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\in\mathbb{Z}^{M\times N_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\mathbb{Z}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\mathbb{Z}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a fixed <math id="A5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{1}\in\mathcal{D}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{1}\in\mathcal{D}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the set of values <math id="A5.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\boldsymbol{i}_{1},\boldsymbol{i}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\boldsymbol{i}_{1},\boldsymbol{i}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> can take
while varying <math id="A5.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{2}\in\mathcal{D}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{2}\in\mathcal{D}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of <math id="A5.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="A5.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A5.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}_{\phi,\boldsymbol{i}_{0}}(\boldsymbol{i}_{1})=\left\{\phi(\boldsymbol{i}_{1},\boldsymbol{i}_{2}):\boldsymbol{i}_{2}\in\mathcal{D}_{2}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Im</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}_{\phi,\boldsymbol{i}_{0}}(\boldsymbol{i}_{1})=\left\{\phi(\boldsymbol{i}_{1},\boldsymbol{i}_{2}):\boldsymbol{i}_{2}\in\mathcal{D}_{2}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p7.3" class="ltx_p">Relaxation finds <math id="A5.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}_{\phi,\boldsymbol{i}_{i}}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Im</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}_{\phi,\boldsymbol{i}_{i}}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})</annotation></semantics></math> and requires it to be a tile
so that it can be expressed in the tile IR.
Since <math id="A5.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is affine and the domain of <math id="A5.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a box,
<math id="A5.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}_{\phi,\boldsymbol{i}_{i}}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Im</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}_{\phi,\boldsymbol{i}_{i}}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})</annotation></semantics></math> is a zonotope for all <math id="A5.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which are more general than tiles (axis-aligned boxes).
We place a constraint on <math id="A5.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure that <math id="A5.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}_{\phi,\boldsymbol{i}_{i}}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Im</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}_{\phi,\boldsymbol{i}_{i}}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})</annotation></semantics></math> is a tile:
each column of <math id="A5.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> has at most one non-zero entry.
We refer to this constraint as <math id="A5.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> being <em id="A5.p7.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">1-sparse</em>.
For example, in Figure <a href="#A3.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ C.2. Privatization ‣ Appendix C Step-By-Step Algorithm Application on Motivational Example ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, the statement <span id="A5.p7.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max_local</span>
has an array access <span id="A5.p7.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">inp[i, j1 * 2 + j2]</span>. Its access function is</p>
<table id="A5.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi((i,j_{0}),(j_{1}))=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\
0&amp;2\\
\end{pmatrix}(i,j_{0})^{T}+\begin{pmatrix}0\\
2\\
\end{pmatrix}(j_{1})^{T}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi((i,j_{0}),(j_{1}))=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\
0&amp;2\\
\end{pmatrix}(i,j_{0})^{T}+\begin{pmatrix}0\\
2\\
\end{pmatrix}(j_{1})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p7.4" class="ltx_p">so <math id="A5.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is 1-sparse. If <math id="A5.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is not 1-sparse, tensorization shrinks <math id="A5.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
by moving loops from <math id="A5.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A5.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which removes rows from the top of <math id="A5.p7.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, until <math id="A5.p7.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is 1-sparse.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}_{\phi,\boldsymbol{i}_{i}}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Im</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}_{\phi,\boldsymbol{i}_{i}}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})</annotation></semantics></math> produces a tile, we use the notation <math id="A5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})</annotation></semantics></math> instead
to express that it is a valid <em id="A5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tile access function</em>.
Relaxation rewrites each <math id="A5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}[\phi_{k}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o},\boldsymbol{i}_{i})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}[\phi_{k}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o},\boldsymbol{i}_{i})]</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation <a href="#A5.E11" title="In Appendix E Neptune IR Syntax and Translation ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>
into a tile access <math id="A5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}[\Phi_{k}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}[\Phi_{k}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})]</annotation></semantics></math>, producing</p>
<table id="A5.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}[\Phi_{0}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})]=F(X_{1}[\Phi_{1}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})],\dots,X_{K}[\Phi_{K}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})])" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}[\Phi_{0}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})]=F(X_{1}[\Phi_{1}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})],\dots,X_{K}[\Phi_{K}(\boldsymbol{i}_{o})])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dimension Order Reconciliation.</span>
Dimension order reconciliation has been described in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Translating from Loop-scalar IR to Tile IR ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
It works on an <span id="A5.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">einops</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>)</cite> expression such as <span id="A5.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">c r -&gt; r c</span>,
which we can derive from the output of the last step
(Equation <a href="#A5.E12" title="In Appendix E Neptune IR Syntax and Translation ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> and the <math id="A5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> matrix of access functions).
Since <math id="A5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is 1-sparse, every non-zero column of <math id="A5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiple of a standard basis vector:</p>
<table id="A5.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex67"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\forall j\in\{1,\dots,|\boldsymbol{i}_{i}|\}\text{ s.t. }V_{\cdot{}j}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> s.t. </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall j\in\{1,\dots,|\boldsymbol{i}_{i}|\}\text{ s.t. }V_{\cdot{}j}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.Ex68"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\exists i(j)\in\{1,\dots,M\},\lambda_{j}\in\mathbb{Z}{},\quad V_{\cdot{}j}=\lambda_{j}\vec{e_{i(j)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\exists i(j)\in\{1,\dots,M\},\lambda_{j}\in\mathbb{Z}{},\quad V_{\cdot{}j}=\lambda_{j}\vec{e_{i(j)}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p9.2" class="ltx_p">The mapping <math id="A5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i(j)</annotation></semantics></math> relates a column of <math id="A5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> (corresponds to a loop in <math id="A5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{i}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{i}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>)
to a standard basis vector (corresponds to a dimension in the tile),
We use this mapping to find a dimension order for each access function <math id="A5.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation <a href="#A5.E12" title="In Appendix E Neptune IR Syntax and Translation ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>,
and assemble them into an <span id="A5.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">einops</span> expression.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Additional Evaluation</h2>

<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1. </span>Compilation Statistics</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide some statistics Neptune’s compilation pipeline
when generating kernels we have evaluated in Section <a href="#S8" title="8. Evaluation ‣ Neptune: Advanced ML Operator Fusion for Locality and Parallelism on GPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.
For these evaluated operators,
Neptune with 128 autotuning iterations takes 1.5 to 10 minutes to run on each setup.
Triton-based tile optimization takes 50% to 90% of the time (depending on the input shape).
Neptune’s template-guided optimization (rolling/split-k update) takes
5% to 10% of the time (does not depend on input shape),
and the rest is TVM autotuning search algorithm.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.2. </span>Operator Evaluation</h3>

